            <ArticleTitle>A rare case of tuberculosis of the midfoot.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>327-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Despite the increasing prevalence of tuberculosis in developed countries, tuberculous osteomyelitis of the foot remains an uncommon complication. The relative rarity of such a diagnosis means that delays are often incurred before clinicians can correctly identify the pathogen and therefore instigate appropriate chemotherapy. The authors present a case of midfoot pain secondary to tuberculous osteomyelitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
